      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a clear, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo truly understand what water is, we can look at it through a few different lenses: its chemical makeup, its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to living things.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Structure (H₂O)\nScientifically, a molecule of water is made up of three atoms:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms** (H)\n*   **One oxygen atom** (O)\n\nThis is why water’s chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. The atoms are held together by strong chemical bonds. Because of the way these atoms interact, water molecules have a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This makes water a **polar molecule**, which is the secret behind many of its amazing properties.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and use to wash.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils or evaporates at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Unique and Amazing Properties\nWater behaves very differently from most other liquids on Earth. Some of its most important properties include:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its polar charge, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries vital nutrients through our bodies and minerals through the soil.\n*   **Ice Floats:** When most liquids freeze, their molecules pack tightly together, making the solid form heavier than the liquid. Water is the opposite. When water freezes, its molecules form a crystal structure that takes up *more* space, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float in your drink, and why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish to survive the winter underneath.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n*   **"
